<p class="lead">
  Sharing a photo publicly — a portfolio preview, a proof for a client, an image meant to be attributed if reposted — often calls for a visible mark showing where it came from. Here's how to add one without an image editor.
</p>

<h2>Watermark a photo in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/image-watermark/">Image Watermark tool</a> on keptlocal stamps text onto a photo with a live preview, entirely on your device.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your photo onto the tool.</li>
  <li>Type the watermark text.</li>
  <li>Pick a color and a position — a diagonal center mark, or a corner/edge placement.</li>
  <li>Adjust size and opacity while watching the live preview.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Download</strong>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Diagonal center vs. corner placement</h2>
<p>
  A diagonal watermark across the center of the frame is the standard choice for stock photo previews and portfolio proofs, for a specific reason: it can't be cropped out without also cutting away the photo's main subject, which sits in the center of most compositions. A corner placement is much easier to crop off entirely, which makes it a weaker deterrent — but it's also far less visually intrusive, which is the right tradeoff when the goal is attribution (a website URL, a handle) rather than actively discouraging reuse.
</p>

<h2>Getting the opacity right</h2>
<p>
  Too low, and the mark is barely visible — easy to miss, and not much of a deterrent either. Too high, and it competes with the photo itself for attention, defeating the purpose of showing the work in the first place. Somewhere around 20–30% opacity tends to be the sweet spot for a mark that's clearly visible without seriously interfering with viewing the image — adjust from there depending on whether the priority is subtlety or visibility.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your photo</h2>
<p>
  The watermark is drawn directly onto the image using the Canvas API — your photo is never uploaded. Open DevTools → Network while using the tool and you'll see no outbound requests, which matters if the photo itself is something you'd rather not send through a third-party server before it's even published.
</p>

